Azure Enterprise App RBAC & PIM Report
Overview

PowerShell automation to generate an Azure RBAC and PIM permission report for Enterprise Applications (Service Principals) across Azure subscriptions and Management Groups.

The script uses the Enterprise Application Object ID as the principal identifier, avoiding display-name ambiguity.

Input

CSV file with the following columns:

subscription,subscriptionid,Enterprise-app-object-id
Connectivity,8a79fe1c-45c1-4fb6-81df-5fa1b9f0a3d8,11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111
Dev-Test,b14faae7-cd5f-4fdb-857d-7b55a3131809,22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222

Input fields
Field	Description
subscription	Azure subscription name
subscriptionid	Azure Subscription ID
Enterprise-app-object-id	Microsoft Entra Service Principal Object ID

Important: Enterprise-app-object-id must be the Service Principal Object ID, not the Application/Client ID.

Prerequisites
PowerShell Modules
Install-Module Az.Accounts -Scope CurrentUser
Install-Module Az.Resources -Scope CurrentUser

Azure Access

The executing account requires read access to:

Target Azure subscriptions
Azure RBAC assignments and role definitions
PIM role eligibility and assignment schedules
Management Groups and their hierarchy
Configuration

Update the paths at the beginning of the script:

$InputCsv  = "C:\Temp\Get-permission.csv"
$OutputCsv = "C:\Temp\test-out.csv"

Execution

Run from PowerShell:

.\Get-EnterpriseApp-RBAC-PIM.ps1


Authenticate when prompted by Connect-AzAccount.

Checks Performed

The report evaluates:

Subscription Normal / Permanent RBAC
Subscription PIM Eligible
Subscription PIM Active / Time-Bound
Management Group Normal / Permanent RBAC
Management Group PIM Eligible
Management Group PIM Active / Time-Bound

Management Group assignments are reported as inherited permissions where applicable.

Output

The generated CSV contains:

Subscription information
Enterprise Application name and Object ID
Application / Client ID
Role name and role definition
Assignment ID
Assignment type and status
RBAC scope and scope type
Inheritance information
Assignment source
PIM start/end dates
Management Group information
Processing status

Example:

InputSubscription : Connectivity
SubscriptionId    : 8a79fe1c-45c1-4fb6-81df-5fa1b9f0a3d8
ObjectType        : ServicePrincipal
ObjectId          : 11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111
RoleName          : Contributor
AssignmentType    : Normal / Permanent
AssignmentStatus  : Active
ScopeType         : Subscription
Inherited         : No
Status             : SUCCESS - NORMAL RBAC

Status Values

Common successful statuses:

SUCCESS - NORMAL RBAC
SUCCESS - PIM ELIGIBLE
SUCCESS - PIM ACTIVE
SUCCESS - MG NORMAL RBAC
SUCCESS - MG PIM ELIGIBLE
SUCCESS - MG PIM ACTIVE


Common non-success statuses:

OBJECT NOT FOUND
NO ROLE / PIM ASSIGNMENT
ERROR SETTING SUBSCRIPTION CONTEXT

Operational Notes
The script uses Object ID rather than display name for reliable Service Principal identification.
Exact duplicate result rows are removed before export.
Role definitions and Management Group relationships are cached to reduce repeated API calls.
Management Group checks are skipped if the executing account cannot retrieve Management Group information.
The output contains authorization information and should be handled according to organizational security requirements.
Recommended Repository Structure
Azure-EnterpriseApp-RBAC-PIM/
